  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evaluation relies exclusively on census-tract-level US commuting data, which may not capture finer-grained urban mobility patterns or non-work travel dynamics.",
      "what": "This study evaluated four human mobility generation models across 2,265 US counties using census-tract commuting data, introducing mutual information and spatial shift metrics to quantify geographic domain shift via linear mixed-effects regression. The analysis reveals significant spatial heterogeneity and directional asymmetry in cross-regional model performance, proving that domain shift components independently drive model transferability.",
      "why": "Researchers can now isolate geographic structural differences from algorithm performance when evaluating GeoAI models, preventing misleading transferability assessments across disparate regions."
    }
  },
